export const timeline = [
 ['Early settlement','Before the 10th century','Life on the floodplain','Vikramapura emerged in the river country of southeastern Bengal. Rivers served as routes for travel and exchange, while floods and shifting channels left a complicated record of earlier settlement. Survey and excavation help locate occupation more precisely than later traditions alone.','archaeology'],
 ['Chandra & Buddhist Bengal','10th–11th centuries','A royal seat and a world of learning','Chandra copper-plate records name Vikramapura as a royal seat. Atish Dipankar, associated with nearby Bajrayogini, belonged to a world of scholarship that connected Bengal with Buddhist centres across Asia. Remains at Raghurampur and Nateshwar help put that intellectual history in a local landscape.','archaeology'],
 ['Varman & Sena','11th–13th centuries','A seat claimed by successive rulers','The Varman and Sena dynasties continued to be associated with Vikramapura. Inscriptions and land grants provide evidence of political authority, but historical accounts differ on the exact timing of some transitions. Rampal preserves place names and traditions connected with this period.','archaeology'],
 ['Sultanate','13th–16th centuries','An inscription anchors the story','The brick mosque at Qadi Kasba offers a firmly dated point in the medieval landscape. Its entrance inscription records 1483, during the Bengal Sultanate. Nearby traditions about Baba Adam belong to the site’s religious memory and are distinct from the inscription’s date.','mosque'],
 ['Mughal Bengal','17th century','Defending a river approach','Bikrampur remained a place name in Mughal administration. At present-day Munshiganj, Mir Jumla’s fort was part of a network of river defences guarding approaches to Dhaka. The raised platform at Idrakpur is the most recognisable feature of the surviving fort.','fort'],
 ['Colonial Bengal','18th–20th centuries','Estates, villages and new institutions','Estate houses, village temples and educational institutions show different sides of life during colonial rule. The nineteenth-century spires at Shyamsiddhi and Sonarang are material records of local patronage. Jagadish Chandra Bose’s family home at Rarikhal links the area to a later history of science and education.','district'],
 ['Liberation War','1971','Resistance and loss','Banglapedia records local resistance in March 1971 and cooperation with people in neighbouring Narayanganj. It also documents killings in Gazaria and at Keoar. Remembering the war here includes the experiences of river settlements and rural families, alongside national events.','district'],
 ['Modern Munshiganj','1984 to present','A district on a changing river corridor','Munshiganj became a district in 1984. Current interpretations of its past draw on modern archaeological work, while the opening of the Padma Bridge in 2022 brought a fixed crossing to the Mawa corridor. Both developments have changed how the region is encountered today.','district'],
];

